## 10. Retry Strategy

Retry behavior should not be based only on whether an exception occurred.

Ask:

> If exactly the same request is executed again later, is there a reasonable possibility that it will succeed?

### 10.1 Generally Retryable

```text
HTTP 429
HTTP 502
HTTP 503
HTTP 504
Connection reset
Temporary network failure
Upstream timeout
Database deadlock
Temporary service unavailable
```

### 10.2 Generally Not Retryable

```text
Invalid parameter
Missing business parameter
Account not found
Permission denied
Business rule violation
Invalid authentication
Programming error
Unexpected KeyError
Unexpected NoneType failure
```

### 10.3 Retryability Matrix

| Error | Retry | Action |
|---|---|---|
| Missing parameter | No | Correct parameter |
| Invalid account ID | No | Correct parameter |
| Account not found | No | Ask user / change parameter |
| Business rule violation | No | Inform user |
| HTTP 429 | Yes | Retry with backoff |
| HTTP 502/503/504 | Yes | Retry with backoff |
| Upstream timeout | Yes | Retry with backoff |
| DB deadlock | Usually | Retry |
| Permission denied | No | Correct authorization |
| Authentication failure | Usually no | Re-authenticate |
| Programming error | No | Internal investigation |

---

## 11. Retry Should Usually Occur Inside the Server First

Infrastructure retries should usually not be delegated immediately to the LLM.

Avoid:

```text
Tool
 ↓
Upstream timeout
 ↓
ToolError
 ↓
LLM retries tool
```

Prefer:

```text
Tool
 ↓
Upstream call
 ↓
Timeout
 ↓
Server retry #1
 ↓
Server retry #2
 ↓
Still failed
 ↓
UpstreamError
 ↓
ToolError
 ↓
Model decides next action
```

Recommended retry strategy:

```text
bounded retry
+
exponential backoff
+
jitter
```

For example:

```python
MAX_RETRIES = 2
```

Do not implement unlimited retries.

FastMCP 3.4.5 also provides middleware capabilities that can be useful for centralized retry policies.

The important principle remains:

> Dependency retries should be explicit, bounded, and restricted to known transient failures.

---

## 12. Timeout Management

Every external dependency should have an explicit timeout.

Avoid:

```python
result = await call_upstream()
```

without a timeout policy.

Prefer:

```python
result = await asyncio.wait_for(
    call_upstream(),
    timeout=10,
)
```

or configure the HTTP client directly:

```python
Timeout(
    connect=2,
    read=8,
    write=5,
)
```

FastMCP also supports tool-level timeout controls.

These two timeout layers solve different problems:

```text
Dependency Timeout
      ↓
Limits individual upstream calls
```

```text
Tool Timeout
      ↓
Limits overall tool execution
```

A production server may use both.

Without explicit timeout boundaries:

```text
Upstream hangs
      ↓
Tool hangs
      ↓
FastMCP request hangs
      ↓
MCP request hangs
      ↓
Client eventually times out
```

This makes root-cause diagnosis significantly harder.

---

## 13. Internal Errors Must Be Sanitized

Avoid:

```python
except Exception as exc:
    raise ToolError(str(exc))
```

This may expose:

```text
Database hostnames
Internal URLs
SQL statements
Table names
Stack traces
File paths
AWS resources
Vendor endpoints
Customer identifiers
Token fragments
Implementation details
```

Instead separate:

```text
Server Diagnostics
```

from:

```text
Client-Visible Error
```

Example:

```python
except Exception:
    error_id = str(uuid.uuid4())

    logger.exception(
        "Unexpected MCP tool error",
        extra={
            "error_id": error_id
        }
    )

    raise ToolError(
        "Unable to complete the request. "
        f"Reference: {error_id}"
    )
```

Internally, this should still be classified as:

```text
category = internal
expected = false
```

FastMCP's error-detail masking capabilities should also be considered as defense in depth.

However:

> Error masking should not replace proper application-level sanitization.

---

## 14. Logging Recommendation

Server logs should contain enough information for debugging.

Recommended fields:

```text
timestamp
correlation_id
request_id
tool_name
error_category
error_code
exception_type
upstream_service
retry_count
latency
stack_trace
```

Client-visible errors should contain only safe information:

```text
error code
safe message
retryability
recommended action
correlation ID
```

Example:

```text
SERVER LOG
--------------------

error_id:
123456

tool:
get_holdings

category:
internal

exception:
KeyError

stack_trace:
...

upstream_service:
account-api


CLIENT / MODEL
--------------------

code:
INTERNAL_ERROR

message:
Unable to complete request.

retryable:
false

correlation_id:
123456
```

Centralized FastMCP error-handling middleware may be used for common logging and observability behavior.

Application-specific error classification should still occur at the appropriate application boundary.

